WEBVTT

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:22.961
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. On today's episode — a Bluetooth flaw in aftermarket car alarms that could hand two-point-two million vehicles' door locks to an attacker, and our Deep Dive question: should a small-town arts venue like Williams Lake's build its digital storytelling around vertical video clips, or around a full-length archive it actually owns? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:38.936 --> 00:01:01.552
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Monday, July twenty-seventh, twenty twenty-six. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Arts, Culture &amp; Digital Storytelling.

00:01:02.552 --> 00:01:14.200
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're looking at how music, performance, and story move through this region when the tools for making them keep changing faster than the venues do — festivals, community radio, digital archives, all of it.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:45.338
<v Riley>Quick weather check before we get into it. Out at Horsefly Lake it's twelve degrees this morning with mainly clear skies, heading to a high of twenty-two and a low of eleven. Tomorrow brings dense drizzle, about two millimetres of precipitation. Across the region — 100 Mile House is sitting at eleven with mainly clear skies, Williams Lake at twelve and overcast, Quesnel at eleven and partly cloudy, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Redstone is sitting at nine with partly cloudy skies.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:52.082
<v Casey>Good day for anyone hauling gear to an outdoor stage, in other words. Let's get into what's actually happening.

00:01:53.482 --> 00:02:18.378
<v Riley>We'll start with a security story that matters if you drive one of roughly two-point-two million vehicles fitted with certain aftermarket alarm systems. TechRadar reports that researchers have found dealer-installed KARR and SWDS security systems can be hijacked over Bluetooth — a vulnerability that lets an attacker remotely unlock doors and, in some configurations, prevent the vehicle from starting at all.

00:02:19.603 --> 00:02:24.219
<v Casey>So the anti-theft device becomes the theft vector. There's a joke in there somewhere.

00:02:25.365 --> 00:02:55.797
<v Riley>It's a real one, though — these are systems marketed specifically as protection, installed by dealerships as an upsell, and the flaw sits in a wireless protocol that predates the tools attackers now have available. For rural drivers already dealing with patchy cell coverage and long tow distances if something goes sideways on Highway 97, this is one more reason to ask a dealership exactly what's inside the box before you pay extra for it. Two-point-two million vehicles is not a small recall footprint.

00:02:56.997 --> 00:03:15.813
<v Casey>Sticking with vehicles behaving badly — the Vancouver Sun reports a fifty-three-year-old driver crashed a motor home through the perimeter fence at Abbotsford International Airport this past weekend and ended up driving onto an active runway. Police say the driver wasn't impaired. Beyond that, there's not much explanation on offer.

00:03:17.036 --> 00:03:21.492
<v Riley>That's the kind of story that answers no questions and somehow raises several new ones.

00:03:22.022 --> 00:03:25.398
<v Casey>Airport security is presumably having a longer Monday than we are.

00:03:26.598 --> 00:03:49.566
<v Riley>On the housing front, Business Insider profiled a couple who got priced out of the Hawaii market entirely and instead bought a home in rural Japan — about twenty-five thousand US dollars for a place with an ocean view, in a country that's been actively trying to repopulate its shrinking countryside by making vacant rural homes cheap and available to buyers willing to renovate.

00:03:51.057 --> 00:04:17.441
<v Casey>Japan's "akiya" listings have been doing this for years, because depopulation there is decades ahead of where we are. It's worth noting the Cariboo Regional District doesn't have anything like a formal program to match empty housing stock with willing buyers, even though the underlying math — an aging population, people priced out of expensive coastal centres, vacant homes sitting unused — rhymes with what Williams Lake and Quesnel have quietly been dealing with for years.

00:04:18.606 --> 00:04:28.646
<v Riley>Different country, same spreadsheet, as you'd put it. It's not our theme today, but it's a story worth flagging because it shows a policy tool other places have built that we haven't.

00:04:29.846 --> 00:04:50.854
<v Casey>Now for something considerably lighter. A newsletter making the rounds this week featured what's being called "the hair dryer question" — an internet bit where someone explains a hair dryer using increasingly absurd, deliberately non-explanatory logic. "It's air, now featuring heat." No actual mechanism explained, no attempt to be helpful — the joke is the refusal to explain.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:05:05.904
<v Riley>Comedy writers have understood that trick forever — the deadpan non-answer is funnier than the real one. It's also, weirdly, a decent snapshot of an entire internet storytelling genre right now: meme logic over information.

00:05:07.104 --> 00:05:27.056
<v Casey>That same newsletter comes from Tod Maffin, the Canadian broadcaster we've referenced a few times this year for building an independent audience through a newsletter and podcast after leaving CBC. This particular item was mostly self-promotion — merch links, podcast links, the usual creator-economy scaffolding that comes with an independent media operation.

00:05:28.284 --> 00:05:34.420
<v Riley>We'll come back to him properly in the Deep Dive — his model is actually load-bearing for where we're headed today.

00:05:36.478 --> 00:06:02.254
<v Casey>Also worth noting — the Marginalian published a reflective essay this week on love, mortality, and what it means to keep creating anyway. It's less a news item and more a meditation, written by someone who's kept an ad-free, reader-funded publication alive for two decades on patronage alone. Not our usual roundup material, but it's a quiet case study in sustaining independent creative work without ads or algorithms — which, again, threads back to today's theme.

00:06:03.556 --> 00:06:10.436
<v Riley>That's the full slate for today's roundup. No corrections to deliver this time around, so let's move straight into who we're spotlighting.

00:06:11.628 --> 00:06:17.844
<v Casey>Speaking of independent creative work sustained on community support — that's a good hinge into where we're going next.

00:06:19.244 --> 00:06:42.668
<v Riley>The Central Cariboo Arts and Culture Society runs the Arts Centre, the region's grants programs, and their Performances in the Park series — free live music every Thursday evening this summer at Boitano Park. No ticket, no cover charge, just a lawn chair and whoever's playing that week. Lineups change weekly, and you can check centralcaribooarts.com to see who's up before you head down.

00:06:43.272 --> 00:06:53.032
<v Casey>Free live music in a public park, run by a local society, is about as uncomplicated a good thing as a community can offer. No app required, no algorithm deciding whether you see it.

00:06:53.532 --> 00:07:06.252
<v Riley>Which is actually the perfect setup for what we want to dig into today — because the tension between "uncomplicated good thing happening in a park" and "everything else in media chasing a feed" is basically the whole conversation.

00:07:07.652 --> 00:07:36.756
<v Riley>The Verge had a piece this week on what they're calling the vertical video takeover. Netflix is now building a vertical, scrollable feed right inside its own app, layered on top of its regular widescreen library. Their January earnings call laid out plans to expand vertical video, podcasts, and even licensed short-form content from Paramount Skydance. The company that built its brand on cinematic, sixteen-by-nine prestige drama is now designing around a phone held upright.

00:07:37.293 --> 00:07:52.013
<v Casey>Netflix chasing TikTok's format tells you something important. It's not that audiences suddenly decided they like worse video. It's that engagement metrics reward a specific shape, and now even the biggest studio on the planet is bending its own product to fit it.

00:07:52.541 --> 00:08:19.645
<v Riley>Or it tells you the audience already moved and Netflix is just catching up to where people actually are. A HubSpot report from last year found vertical short-form was the single most-used and most successful content format in all of marketing, full stop. That's not a taste judgment — it's a distribution fact. And for a musician or a small theatre company way out here, that fact matters more than any critic's opinion about format snobbery.

00:08:20.282 --> 00:08:38.426
<v Casey>Distribution fact for whom, though. A fifteen-second vertical clip of a three-chord hook works because it's engineered to be consumed in fifteen seconds. Live theatre isn't built that way. A folk trio playing a forty-minute set at Boitano Park doesn't compress into a hook and a caption without losing what actually makes it good.

00:08:38.826 --> 00:09:01.730
<v Riley>Nobody's proposing you replace the performance with the clip. The clip is the door, not the room. A thirty-second vertical teaser from a Thursday night in the park puts that band in front of people in Kamloops or Prince George who've never heard of Horsefly Lake in their lives — and some fraction of those people show up next summer, or buy the record, or at least follow along.

00:09:02.273 --> 00:09:19.833
<v Casey>Some fraction. What's the actual conversion rate on that fraction? Because the honest answer, in every serious study of viral reach, is that most of those impressions evaporate without ever turning into a ticket sale or an album purchase. Vertical video builds impressions. It doesn't reliably build audiences.

00:09:20.373 --> 00:09:44.165
<v Riley>That's true of any single clip in isolation. It's much less true of a body of work posted consistently over an entire season. Tod Maffin's model — the one we flagged back in June — is the proof that the compounding version actually works. He didn't go viral once and coast. He built weekly output on a platform he controlled, over years, and it became a genuinely large, durable reach.

00:09:44.743 --> 00:10:10.439
<v Casey>We got into this a couple of weeks back, and the thing that held up then still holds now. Maffin's an established broadcaster with two decades of CBC credibility behind him before he ever hit publish on a newsletter. A garage band in Likely doesn't start with that kind of built-in trust. The cold-start problem we talked about in June is still exactly the cold-start problem in July — nothing about vertical video changes who has an audience on day one.

00:10:10.839 --> 00:10:32.351
<v Riley>Fine — but the tools for surviving that cold start keep getting cheaper, and that part actually is new. Take the PianoPi story from this week — a hobbyist built an entire robot piano player out of a Raspberry Pi and coloured light strips, purely because they wanted to see it work. No studio budget, no label, no algorithm involved in the decision to build it.

00:10:32.995 --> 00:10:55.091
<v Casey>And it's living quietly in a hobbyist forum on XDA Developers, seen by maybe a few thousand people who already follow Raspberry Pi projects. That's the actual tension here. PianoPi has zero engagement pressure and correspondingly tiny reach. Vertical video has enormous reach and constant, structural engagement pressure. Nobody's built the version of digital storytelling that gets both at once.

00:10:55.712 --> 00:11:15.648
<v Riley>Maybe that's the wrong way to frame it as a binary, though. Nothing stops a Cariboo musician from doing the PianoPi thing — building something odd and personal because they want to — and separately posting a vertical clip of it existing. The tools aren't competing with each other. They're doing two different jobs: one's the workshop, one's the door.

00:11:16.048 --> 00:11:40.408
<v Casey>Except time and attention are finite, and that's the part that gets lost in "just do both." Every hour a musician here spends learning to shoot, cut, and caption vertical clips for a feed is an hour not spent rehearsing, or building the instrument, or actually being present in the community they're supposedly representing. The platform doesn't just want your content — it wants your production habits reorganized around its shape.

00:11:41.007 --> 00:11:54.071
<v Riley>That's a real cost. I'll grant that much without argument. But refusing the format entirely has a cost too — it's just invisible, because it looks like nothing happening rather than something visibly being lost.

00:11:54.591 --> 00:12:09.415
<v Casey>Refusing outright isn't the only alternative, though. There's a third option nobody in the Verge piece even considers: build the archive and the audience on infrastructure the community actually owns, and let the platform clip be a byproduct of that, rather than the goal itself.

00:12:09.815 --> 00:12:12.783
<v Riley>Meaning what, concretely, for somewhere like the Arts Centre?

00:12:13.058 --> 00:12:50.274
<v Casey>Meaning a venue — the Arts Centre here in Williams Lake, or a community hall in Quesnel — fixes one camera on a tripod every Thursday during Performances in the Park and records the full set. That recording goes somewhere the community actually controls, whether that's a shared local archive or something like Internet Archive's free hosting, which we talked about back in early July for exactly this kind of use. Then, if someone wants to cut a vertical highlight from that footage for whatever platform is popular that month, fine — go ahead. But the underlying record doesn't live or die by whether TikTok still exists in its current form five years from now.

00:12:50.927 --> 00:13:06.767
<v Riley>That actually threads the needle. The full-length recording becomes the durable asset. The vertical clip is disposable marketing sitting on top of something permanent, instead of the vertical clip being the only thing that ever existed of that performance.

00:13:07.414 --> 00:13:27.750
<v Casey>Which flips the entire relationship most small arts organizations currently have with social media. Right now, most of them treat the platform post as the primary record and everything else as an afterthought — if it exists on Instagram, it exists; if not, it's gone. This inverts that. The community owns the master copy. The platform only ever gets the trailer.

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:49.658
<v Riley>And that's genuinely different ground than what we were arguing back in June about audience-building generally. That conversation was about whether platform-independent distribution could work at all for rural creators. This is a step further — it's platform-independent documentation, with distribution treated as an optional, downstream layer rather than the whole strategy.

00:13:50.191 --> 00:14:08.263
<v Casey>It still needs somebody to actually show up with the camera every single week for a whole season, though — which runs straight into the discipline problem we hit a few weeks ago talking about twenty years of fragmented festival archives. Good intentions in May, forgotten camera by July, nobody quite sure whose job it was.

00:14:08.913 --> 00:14:24.377
<v Riley>Sure, but the bar here is meaningfully lower than a full archival project with metadata and cataloguing. One fixed camera, one memory card, one upload after the show. It's not asking for a trained archivist — it's asking for a habit, and a fairly small one.

00:14:24.925 --> 00:14:38.125
<v Casey>A habit that still competes with everything else a volunteer-run arts society already juggles on a Thursday night in the middle of festival season, when the same three people are also selling merch, running sound, and locking up the park afterward.

00:14:38.525 --> 00:15:04.085
<v Riley>Which is a legitimate operational concern, but it's answering a different question than the one the Verge piece actually raises. Their question is whether vertical video is homogenizing culture around a single algorithmic shape. The answer, from where we're sitting, is: only if you let the clip become the whole story. If the full, unedited recording exists somewhere durable, the vertical cut is just packaging on top of it.

00:15:04.715 --> 00:15:31.899
<v Casey>I'll take the packaging framing. What I won't concede is that packaging stays neutral once it exists. The moment a band knows the fifteen-second cut is what actually travels and gets watched, some of them start writing songs with a hook engineered for the first three seconds. That's not hypothetical — it's already well documented in how a lot of commercial pop songwriting has restructured itself around exactly that window, because streaming services surface skip rates in real time.

00:15:32.555 --> 00:15:49.059
<v Riley>That pressure is real for artists chasing platform revenue and algorithmic placement. It's much weaker for someone playing a free Thursday night set in a public park with no monetization attached to any of it. Different economics produce a genuinely different incentive structure.

00:15:49.707 --> 00:16:00.483
<v Casey>For now, maybe. The moment Performances in the Park starts measuring its own success by clip views instead of people actually sitting on the lawn, that incentive shows up here too, and it shows up fast.

00:16:00.883 --> 00:16:17.603
<v Riley>Then don't measure it that way. Measure attendance in the park, measure whether people come back the following Thursday. Let the clip do its one job — pulling in the next new face who wasn't there — without letting it become the metric the organization actually optimizes around.

00:16:18.201 --> 00:16:29.665
<v Casey>That's an organizational discipline question, not a technology question, and discipline is exactly the thing that tends not to survive a change in who happens to be running the society from one year to the next.

00:16:30.195 --> 00:16:51.851
<v Riley>Which argues for building the discipline into the technical default from the start, rather than relying on a policy memo nobody reads twice. Record everything full-length first, automatically. Treat any short clip as a secondary export, something that gets pulled from the archive afterward — not something shot separately, under separate pressure, in the moment.

00:16:52.351 --> 00:17:11.983
<v Casey>If Central Cariboo Arts wanted to actually test that idea, it wouldn't take a grant application or a funding cycle. One volunteer, one tripod, one Thursday, uploaded somewhere outside any social feed before anyone even thinks about cutting a vertical version out of it. See if the habit survives a full month before anyone talks about scaling it up.

00:17:12.605 --> 00:17:18.461
<v Riley>That's a genuinely testable experiment, and it doesn't require anyone's permission to start it next Thursday.

00:17:18.986 --> 00:17:39.506
<v Casey>The open question that doesn't go away, though, is whether a full-length Thursday-night recording, sitting quietly in a plain archive with no algorithm pushing it toward anyone, actually gets watched by a single person who wasn't already standing in the park that night. Ownership solves the preservation problem. It does nothing on its own to solve the discovery problem.

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:55.430
<v Riley>No, it doesn't — you're right about that. But it means discovery is the only problem left to solve, instead of both preservation and discovery depending entirely on whichever platform happens to still be operating, under whatever terms, five years from now.

00:17:56.016 --> 00:18:08.048
<v Casey>I'll leave it there, then. Own the master recording, treat the vertical clip as disposable marketing sitting on top of it, and stop treating those two things as though they're the same question with the same solution.

00:18:08.716 --> 00:18:22.068
<v Riley>If anyone running a small venue wants to try the one-camera experiment this summer, or if you've got a correction on anything we said today, the address is feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. We read everything that comes in.

00:18:22.373 --> 00:18:23.501
<v Casey>Have a great rest of your day.

00:18:23.806 --> 00:18:24.982
<v Riley>Have a great rest of your day.